</div>
</div>
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mb-8">
<div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange p-6 rounded-r-xl mb-8 speakable">
<p class="text-lg text-slate-700 italic">A power bank clears Korean customs only when three things line up. The right KC scheme. A safety-confirmation certificate in the Korean importer's name. A label that matches the import declaration. Miss any one, and the shipment waits in bond while the launch date moves.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Korea KC certification for power bank importers starts with a choice of legal route. There are three, and the route changes by product. A power bank follows one route. A USB charger follows another. A wireless charger can trigger a third regulator. Most English guides fold all of this into "KC certification." The importer then discovers the difference at the port.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">This article separates the routes. It is for the brand, importer, or sourcing team with a factory quote in hand. You need to know what to file, who must hold the certificate, and what Korean customs will check. WOWOHCOOL supports KC documentation for OEM orders, and our certification team works through these steps with buyers before tooling starts.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mt-4"><strong>Need a power bank line that already has the documentation trail?</strong> The <a href="/products/power-bank/">OEM power bank catalog</a> covers 5,000 mAh to 27,000 mAh models with CE, FCC, RoHS, and UN38.3 files ready for market-specific work.</p>
</div>
