<p class="text-badge font-black text-brandOrange uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Key Takeaways</p>
<p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm mb-4 speakable">Korea KC certification for power bank importers: B2B rules for safety confirmation vs certification, certificate holder, customs matching, and 2026 changes.</p>
<ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
<li>A power bank is a <strong>safety-confirmation</strong> product under KC 62133-2, not a safety-certification product. It needs testing and a report, but no factory audit.</li>
<li>A USB charger or DC adapter is generally a <strong>safety-certification</strong> product under KC 62368-1. A battery charger with charge-control logic can fall under KC 60335-2-29 instead.</li>
<li>A foreign factory cannot file in its own name. The Korean importer, or a Korean resident agent, must file, and the certificate model and importer names must match the import declaration character for character.</li>
<li>Korean customs checks the certificate through <strong>세관장확인</strong> before release. A test report without a matching safety-confirmation certificate is not enough.</li>
<li>The draft KC 62133-2 revision would add KC 62368-1 whole-unit safety for power banks. The WTO comment period closes on 2026-10-26, and the final effective dates are still undetermined.</li>
</ul>
