<section id="korea-kc-certification-for-power-bank-importers-three-schemes" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">1. Korea KC Certification for Power Bank Importers: Three Schemes</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The KC mark is one label. The legal route behind it is not. Korea's Electrical Appliances and Consumer Products Safety Control Act (전기용품 및 생활용품 안전관리법) sets three levels of obligation. Each level assigns a different amount of work to the factory and the importer.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">This is the Korea entry in our market-access series. The <a href="/blog/japan-pse-meti-charger-importers/">Japan PSE/METI guide</a> follows the same structure for a different regulator.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><thead><tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest"><th class="p-4 text-left">Scheme</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Korean name</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Who tests</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Factory audit</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Typical products</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-slate-600"><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Safety Certification</td><td class="p-4">안전인증</td><td class="p-4">KATS-designated test body</td><td class="p-4">Yes, initial and annual</td><td class="p-4">AC adapters, DC power supplies, many household chargers</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Safety Confirmation</td><td class="p-4">안전확인</td><td class="p-4">KATS-designated test body</td><td class="p-4">No</td><td class="p-4">Lithium-ion batteries and power banks</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Supplier's Declaration</td><td class="p-4">공급자적합성확인</td><td class="p-4">Supplier self-declaration</td><td class="p-4">No</td><td class="p-4">Lower-risk products outside this article's scope</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The distinction matters because the words "KC certified" hide it. A supplier can send a certificate that looks valid and still be on the wrong route for your product. The importer then discovers the mismatch when customs asks for a document the certificate does not represent.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For power banks, the route is Korea safety confirmation. The product sits in the "battery (lithium secondary battery)" category in Enforcement Rule Annex 4. The legal basis for the filing is Article 15 of the Act. The applicable standard is KC 62133-2, the Korean adoption of IEC 62133-2 for portable sealed lithium secondary cells and packs.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">That route does not include a factory audit. It does include model-by-model testing, a safety-confirmation report, and a label. The absence of a factory audit is not a loophole. It shifts the burden onto the importer, who must keep the certificate, the model list, and the label aligned with every shipment.</p>
<h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mt-6 mb-3">Why the scheme matters before you choose a factory</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The scheme decides which documents the factory must prepare and which documents the importer must hold. If you brief a factory for "KC" without naming the scheme, you may get the wrong certificate. It could be a charger-style certificate for a power bank, or a battery report that doesn't cover the finished product.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The correction is cheap before tooling and expensive after. Ask the factory to state the scheme and standard in the quotation, not just the mark. Then ask the test body to confirm the classification in writing for the exact product construction you plan to ship.</p>
