<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">2. Power Bank vs Charger: Which KC Scheme Applies?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Power banks and chargers look like one product family to a buyer. Korean law treats them as different categories, and the difference is not cosmetic.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><thead><tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest"><th class="p-4 text-left">Product</th><th class="p-4 text-left">KC scheme</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Standard</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Factory audit</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Certificate holder</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-slate-600"><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Power bank, DC in and DC out only</td><td class="p-4">Safety Confirmation</td><td class="p-4">KC 62133-2</td><td class="p-4">No</td><td class="p-4">Korean importer or domestic agent</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">USB charger / DC power adapter</td><td class="p-4">Safety Certification</td><td class="p-4">KC 62368-1</td><td class="p-4">Yes</td><td class="p-4">Korean importer or domestic agent</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Battery charger with charge control</td><td class="p-4">Safety Certification</td><td class="p-4">KC 60335-2-29</td><td class="p-4">Yes</td><td class="p-4">Korean importer or domestic agent</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Wireless charger with a radio function</td><td class="p-4">Safety route plus RRA assessment</td><td class="p-4">KC route plus radio-law conformity</td><td class="p-4">Depends on the safety route</td><td class="p-4">Importer or agent, plus RRA applicant</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8">
 <img src="/image/product/power-bank/wop21-67w-power-bank-900w.webp"
 srcset="/image/product/power-bank/wop21-67w-power-bank-800w.webp 800w, /image/product/power-bank/wop21-67w-power-bank-900w.webp 900w"
 sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 798px, (min-width: 768px) 670px, (min-width: 430px) 332px, 277px"
 alt="WOP21 67W power bank with built-in cables, a DC-only power bank that follows Korea safety confirmation"
 width="900" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
 class="rounded-2xl shadow-lg w-full">
 <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 text-center mt-2">A DC-only power bank follows Korea safety confirmation, not safety certification.</p>
</div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The charger split needs care. KC 62368-1 covers audio, video, information, and communication equipment, including DC power supply units rated up to 1 kVA. Korea retired KS C 60950-1 and KS C 60065 at the end of 2022 and moved these products onto KS C IEC 62368-1. The underlying requirements are covered in more detail in our <a href="/blog/charger-safety-standards/">charger safety certification standards</a> guide.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">KC 60335-2-29 covers household and similar battery chargers. KATS Notice 2026-23, issued on 2026-02-05, revised that standard. The previous standard may be applied in parallel until 2027-02-05, after which old certificates must be updated.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The practical dividing line reported by Korean test bodies is charge control. A device that manages charging voltage, current, or an algorithm leans toward KC 60335-2-29. An uncontrolled DC adapter that only supplies power leans toward KC 62368-1. Construction, rated input, and whether the charger is sold with a device can change the answer. The test body's written classification governs. If you are sourcing a GaN charger, the <a href="/products/gan-charger/">GaN charger OEM line</a> shows the form factors and power ranges we build.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If the product also carries Qi2, the WPC product certification sits on top of the KC route. Our <a href="/blog/qi-certification-guide/">Qi certification guide</a> covers that separate process.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A buyer we will call Elena learned this the expensive way. Her team approved a 10,000-unit power bank program on the strength of a "KC certificate" the factory had used for a wall charger. The certificate was real, and it was for the wrong product. The power bank needed a safety-confirmation report under KC 62133-2. The team paid for a second round of testing and missed the retail window by five weeks.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Building a charger and a power bank under one brand?</strong> The <a href="/products/wireless-charger/">Qi2 wireless charger OEM line</a> and the <a href="/service/">OEM/ODM service team</a> can scope both product routes before certification starts.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="korea-kc-certification-for-power-bank-importers-who-files" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">3. Korea KC Certification for Power Bank Importers: Who Files?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The most common Korean market-access mistake is not a failed test. It is a certificate in the wrong name.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><a href="https://lbox.kr/statute/%ec%a0%84%ea%b8%b0%ec%9a%a9%ed%92%88%20%eb%b0%8f%20%ec%83%9d%ed%99%9c%ec%9a%a9%ed%92%88%20%ec%95%88%ec%a0%84%ea%b4%80%eb%a6%ac%eb%b2%95%20%ec%8b%9c%ed%96%89%ea%b7%9c%ec%b9%99/%ec%a0%9c27%ec%a1%b0?statuteType=%ec%82%b0%ec%97%85%ed%86%b5%ec%83%81%eb%b6%80%eb%a0%b9&effectiveDate=2026-04-01&proclamationNumber=%ec%a0%9c+00009%ed%98%b8&proclamationDate=2026-04-01&revisionType=%ec%9d%bc%eb%b6%80%ea%b0%9c%ec%a0%95" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Enforcement Rule Article 27</a> requires the manufacturer or importer to file a safety-confirmation report for each model before shipment or customs clearance. The same article allows a foreign manufacturer to appoint a person resident in Korea as its agent to file. That permission is not a shortcut around the importer. It is a defined role with its own paperwork.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><thead><tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest"><th class="p-4 text-left">Filing route</th><th class="p-4 text-left">What the certificate shows</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Who owns the certificate</th><th class="p-4 text-left">What the importer needs</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-slate-600"><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Korean importer files</td><td class="p-4">Importer and manufacturer</td><td class="p-4">Importer</td><td class="p-4">Business registration, test report, label artwork</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Domestic agent files for the foreign manufacturer</td><td class="p-4">Manufacturer, with agent details</td><td class="p-4">Manufacturer; agent has no ownership</td><td class="p-4">Signed power of attorney, agent proof documents</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Factory tries to file alone without a Korean entity</td><td class="p-4">Not accepted</td><td class="p-4">None</td><td class="p-4">A Korean importer or agent must be appointed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Two consequences follow. First, a foreign factory cannot simply hand you "its KC certificate" and expect your shipment to clear. A Korean importer KC certificate is tied to that importer, not to the factory. If the certificate is in another importer's name, your import declaration will not match. Parallel imports of a model already reported by another importer need a separate exemption procedure with the safety-certification body.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Second, the model name must be identical across the certificate, the purchase order, the invoice, and the label. Not similar. Identical, down to the characters.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A color or case-only derivative may be grouped under a base model. A change in capacity, cell, or circuit creates a new model and a new report.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Changes to the importer's name or address, or to the cell supplier, must be reported within seven days using Annex Form 16. That deadline is easy to miss after a company rename or a warehouse move, and the certificate can become unusable while the product is already in transit.</p>
<h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mt-6 mb-3">Decide the importer of record before tooling</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The importer of record is a commercial decision with a certification consequence. If you plan to sell through a Korean distributor, decide whether that distributor will hold the certificate or whether you will appoint an agent. Put the answer in the project brief before the factory prints a label.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A procurement lead we will call Hana changed distributors three months before launch. The new distributor wanted its own name on the certificate. The factory had already printed 8,000 labels with the old importer's details.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The fix wasn't a reprint alone. It required a change notification, a new label batch, and a customs broker briefing. The product was ready; the paperwork wasn't.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="the-customs-gate-what-must-match-before-release" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">4. The Customs Gate: What Must Match Before Release</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Korean customs does not treat the KC certificate as a filing formality. Power banks are subject to <strong>세관장확인</strong>, the customs-head requirement verification under the Electrical Appliances Act. At import declaration, the safety-confirmation certificate must match the requirement-verification field.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The sequence is fixed:</p>
<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 mb-4"><li>1. Finalize the base model and derivative list.</li><li>2. Collect the factory technical file, cell specification, BMS specification, and UN38.3 test summary.</li><li>3. Send samples to a KATS-designated test body for KC 62133-2 testing.</li><li>4. File the safety-confirmation report and obtain the certificate number.</li><li>5. Print and attach the KC label with every required marking.</li><li>6. Book transport as UN3480 dangerous goods where applicable.</li><li>7. File the import declaration and link the certificate for 세관장확인.</li></ol>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A test report alone does not pass step 7.</p>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-brandOrange pl-4 italic text-slate-600 mb-4"><strong>Expert insight:</strong> A KC certificate is a document with a name on it. If the name is not your importer, the certificate is not yours to use. The certificate must exist, be in the right name, and cover the exact model. If the model name or importer name differs, the shipment is held. If the label is missing, the fix happens in a bonded warehouse, which adds time and cost.</blockquote>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Non-sale imports for research, exhibitions, market testing, or certification can use an exemption certificate (면제확인서) from the Korea Product Safety Management Institute. That certificate must be obtained before customs, not after the goods arrive.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The dangerous-goods side has its own packaging and documentation rules. Our guide to <a href="/blog/lithium-battery-dangerous-goods-packaging-certification/">UN38.3 and lithium battery packaging</a> covers that file.</p>
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8">
 <img src="/image/blog/power-bank/power-bank-container-loading-export.webp"
 srcset="/image/blog/power-bank/power-bank-container-loading-export-900w.webp 900w, /image/blog/power-bank/power-bank-container-loading-export.webp 1600w"
 sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 798px, (min-width: 768px) 670px, (min-width: 430px) 332px, 277px"
 alt="Palletized lithium-ion battery cartons with UN3480 labels staged for export"
 width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
 class="rounded-2xl shadow-lg w-full">
 <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 text-center mt-2">UN3480 labeling and dangerous-goods documents travel with the KC file.</p>
</div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The HS code sits beside this process, not inside it. Korean customs brokers commonly cite HS 8507.60 for lithium-ion accumulators. Duty and VAT treatment depends on the code, origin, and FTA documentation. Our <a href="/blog/import-costs-guide/">import cost and HS code guide</a> covers the duty side; this article stays on the KC side so the two do not compete.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="kc-mark-labeling-what-must-be-on-the-product-before-customs" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">5. KC Mark Labeling: What Must Be on the Product Before Customs</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The label is part of the release gate, not a post-launch design task. Under the Operation Guidelines Annex 23, the product or packaging must carry the following before customs:</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 mb-4"><li>KC mark</li><li>Safety-confirmation report number</li><li>Model name</li><li>Manufacturer and importer name</li><li>Country of manufacture</li><li>Manufacturing date</li><li>Domestic A/S contact</li></ul>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Korean-language manuals and safety warnings are also required. Certification agencies commonly report that machine-translated manuals are rejected, and that the KC mark height is expected to be at least 5 mm. Confirm the final artwork against the current Annex 23 and the test body's instructions, because label reviewers check placement as well as content.</p>
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8 text-center">
 <img src="/image/certifications/kc.svg" alt="Korea KC certification mark" width="498" height="701" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-full max-w-[120px] h-auto object-contain mx-auto mb-3">
 <p class="text-xs text-slate-500">The KC mark, report number, model, and importer name must match before customs.</p>
</div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Three label failures recur:</p>
<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 mb-4"><li>1. The importer name on the label differs from the certificate.</li><li>2. The model string on the label differs from the invoice by a suffix or space.</li><li>3. The label is designed for the retail box but not applied to the product body.</li></ol>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The first two are customs holds. The third is a market-surveillance risk after launch. Treat the label artwork as a controlled document with the same version discipline as the packaging dieline.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="what-changes-in-2026-and-2027" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">6. What Changes in 2026 and 2027</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Two revisions matter for charging products. One is already in force. The other is still a draft.</p>
<h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mt-6 mb-3">KC 62133-2 draft for power banks</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">KATS notified the WTO of a draft revision on 2026-08-27 under <a href="https://epingalert.org/en/Search/Index?domainIds=1&documentSymbol=G/TBT/N/KOR/1378" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">G/TBT/N/KOR/1378</a>, based on KATS Public Notification 2026-248. The comment period closes on 2026-10-26. The draft would:</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 mb-4"><li>Add KC 62368-1 whole-unit safety for power banks and "power-bank-like devices," alongside KC 62133-2.</li><li>Replace the handheld, transportable, and movable categories with a single "portable equipment" definition of 18 kg or less.</li><li>Exclude batteries of 500 Wh or more, drones, automobiles, and products under separate legislation.</li><li>Make the internal short-circuit test a substantive requirement for cells.</li><li>Add overcharge conditions for boost-charging products and a new clause for power-bank-like devices.</li></ul>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The notification does not set final adoption or entry-into-force dates. Industry articles expect 2027 with a grace period, but that expectation is not an official date. Do not put "mandatory July 2027" in a launch plan as if it were confirmed. Put it in the risk column and ask the test body for the latest status.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The practical preparation is the same either way. Confirm the cell and BMS file. Confirm the CB report edition. Ask whether the current power bank certificate will need an amendment or a new application when the final text lands.</p>
<h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mt-6 mb-3">KC 60335-2-29 revision for chargers</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><a href="https://www.kats.go.kr/content.do?cmsid=239&mode=view&cid=26102" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">KATS Notice 2026-23</a> took effect on 2026-02-05. The previous standard may be applied in parallel until 2027-02-05. After that date, old certificates must be updated. If your charger program runs into 2027, budget the update now rather than treating the transition as a launch-time surprise.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="what-does-not-apply-rra-emc-and-the-usb-c-rule" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">7. What Does Not Apply: RRA/EMC and the USB-C Rule</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Two rules are often sold to importers who do not need them. Both deserve a direct answer.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>RRA/EMC for a DC-only power bank.</strong> Korea's Radio Research Agency excludes simple DC-in and DC-out batteries and power banks from radio-law conformity assessment under <a href="https://www.rra.go.kr/ko/license/qnaList_view.do?req_seq=272" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Notice Annex 1</a>. A power bank that only takes DC power and delivers DC power does not need an RRA 적합성평가 for that function. A wireless-charging power bank, a Bluetooth power bank, an AC-output inverter, or a product with another radio function does need a separate assessment. The exemption follows the function, not the product name.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>The USB Type-C rule for power banks.</strong> <a href="https://taiwan.ul.com/gma/korea-mist-announced-usb-type-c-mandatory-requirements-under-kc-emc-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">MSIT Notice 2025-56</a> requires USB Type-C receptacles for wired charging and data transfer on 13 end-product categories, effective 2026-11-06. The list covers mobile phones, tablets, laptops, digital cameras, headphones, headsets, handheld game consoles, portable speakers, e-readers, keyboards, mice, portable navigation devices, and earphones. Power banks and standalone chargers are not on the list. Only devices receiving a new conformity assessment after the deadline are subject to the rule.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">That does not make USB-C irrelevant. It means a supplier should not invoice you for a USB-C KC approval on a power bank as if the rule covered it. Ask which product category the approval is for, and get the answer in writing.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="cost-timeline-and-cb-to-kc-conversion" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">8. Cost, Timeline, and CB to KC Conversion</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">KC costs vary because the scope varies. A safety-confirmation report for a power bank is a different job from a safety-certification file for a charger with a factory audit.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Our certification cost table lists KC at <strong>$1,500-3,000</strong> and <strong>3-5 weeks</strong> for a standard file. Agency quotations that bundle the Korean agent, RF testing, or multiple models can run higher. One widely cited agency range for a power bank program is RMB 20,000-40,000, with wireless or Bluetooth variants above that. Treat both figures as planning ranges, not quotes.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><thead><tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest"><th class="p-4 text-left">Cost driver</th><th class="p-4 text-left">What changes the number</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-slate-600"><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Scheme</td><td class="p-4">Safety certification adds factory audit and annual surveillance</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Radio function</td><td class="p-4">Wireless or Bluetooth adds RRA testing</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Model count</td><td class="p-4">Each capacity, cell, or circuit change can create a new model</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">CB report</td><td class="p-4">A valid IECEE CB report can reduce repeat testing</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Korean agent</td><td class="p-4">Agent fees and power-of-attorney work are separate from lab fees</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Re-tests</td><td class="p-4">Failed safety or EMC tests add both cost and weeks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The CB-to-KC route is the most reliable saving. An IECEE CB report with the Korean national deviations can cut repeat testing and shorten the timeline. Our CB guidance notes that one IEC 62368-1 test can be recognized in more than 60 countries. The report can be converted to KC, CE, UL, or PSE. That can save 30-50% on repeat testing when the report is current and covers the right edition.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Timeline has two layers. The statutory processing period for a safety-confirmation report is seven days. Testing runs separately, and the overall program commonly takes four to eight weeks when documents are complete. A factory that promises "KC in two weeks" is either quoting a conversion with an existing report or omitting a step.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="factory-side-document-checklist" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">9. Factory-Side Document Checklist</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A KC program moves at the speed of the slowest document. Send this list to the factory before the first sample, not after the purchase order.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><thead><tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest"><th class="p-4 text-left">Document</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Why the test body needs it</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-slate-600"><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Business license and factory name/address</td><td class="p-4">Applicant and manufacturer identity</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Product description with photos</td><td class="p-4">Scope and construction review</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Circuit diagram and PCB layout</td><td class="p-4">Safety assessment</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">BOM with critical components</td><td class="p-4">Component certification cross-check</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Cell specification: maker, model, format, capacity</td><td class="p-4">KC 62133-2 scope</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">BMS specification: overcharge, over-discharge, overcurrent, short circuit</td><td class="p-4">Protection design review</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Rated input/output voltage, current, capacity in mAh and Wh</td><td class="p-4">Test conditions</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">IEC 62133-2 CB report, if available</td><td class="p-4">Conversion and test reduction</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">UN38.3 test summary</td><td class="p-4">Transport and customs file</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Korean manual and safety warnings</td><td class="p-4">Label and market-surveillance review</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Label artwork with KC mark, report number, model, importer, A/S contact</td><td class="p-4">Pre-customs marking</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Agent power of attorney, if an agent files</td><td class="p-4">Article 27 filing route</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Two checks catch most problems before they reach the lab. First, compare the model list against the purchase order and the label artwork in one pass. Second, verify that the cell in the BOM is the cell in the CB report and the UN38.3 summary. A cell substitution after certification is a change notification, not a production detail.</p>
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8">
