<section id="five-failure-patterns-that-hold-shipments" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">10. Five Failure Patterns That Hold Shipments</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">These are the patterns we see most often when a Korean launch slips.</p>
<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 mb-4"><li>1. <strong>Wrong scheme.</strong> A power bank is filed as a charger, or a charger is filed as a battery. The test report exists, but it does not match the legal route.</li><li>2. <strong>Certificate in the wrong name.</strong> The factory holds a report from another importer, or the agent files without a signed power of attorney. Customs cannot match the declaration.</li><li>3. <strong>Model mismatch.</strong> The certificate says one model string; the invoice, label, or packaging says another. A suffix or a space is enough to trigger a hold.</li><li>4. <strong>Label after customs.</strong> The product arrives without the KC mark or the report number, and the fix happens in a bonded warehouse.</li><li>5. <strong>Substitution after certification.</strong> The cell, BMS, or circuit changes without a change notification. The certificate no longer covers the shipped product.</li></ol>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A sixth pattern is commercial rather than technical: assuming CE, FCC, or UL substitutes for KC. They don't. Korea runs its own scheme, and a CE certificate is not a Korean market-access document. Our <a href="/blog/certifications-us-eu-guide/">US and EU charger certification guide</a> explains what those marks do cover.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="conclusion" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">Conclusion</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Korea KC certification for power bank importers rewards preparation over speed. The market is reachable for a charger or power bank brand, but the sequence matters. Confirm the scheme first: safety confirmation for a power bank, safety certification for a charger. Then confirm who files, because the certificate must sit with the Korean importer or a properly appointed agent. Then treat the label and the customs match as part of the product, not as paperwork after the product.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The 2026 draft adds a second safety standard for power banks. It doesn't change the sequence. A clean cell and BMS file still matter. So do a current CB report, a Korean manual, and a label that matches the declaration. Those will serve you whether the final date lands in 2027 or later.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you are planning a Korean launch, send the product specification and target model list to our team. We can confirm the likely scheme, the documents the factory needs to prepare, and the certification path before tooling. <a href="/contact/">Request a quote</a> to start.</p>
