<section id="faq" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Does a power bank need KC certification to enter Korea?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Yes. A power bank needs KC compliance before customs release. The route is safety confirmation under KC 62133-2, not safety certification. The importer or a Korean agent files a safety-confirmation report for each model, and the certificate must match the import declaration. A test report alone is not enough. Korean customs checks the certificate through 세관장확인, and a missing or mismatched certificate holds the shipment.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Is a power bank safety certification or safety confirmation?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">A power bank is a safety-confirmation product. It sits in the lithium secondary battery category under Enforcement Rule Annex 4, and the legal basis is Article 15 of the Electrical Appliances and Consumer Products Safety Control Act. Safety confirmation requires testing and a report but no factory audit. A USB charger or DC adapter is generally a safety-certification product under KC 62368-1, which does require a factory audit and annual surveillance.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Can a Chinese factory hold the KC certificate?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">A foreign factory cannot file in its own name without a Korean legal entity. It can appoint a Korean resident agent under Enforcement Rule Article 27, or the Korean importer can file. If the importer files, the certificate commonly shows both importer and manufacturer and ownership sits with the importer. If an agent files, the certificate names the manufacturer and the agent has no ownership. A signed power of attorney is required.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Does a DC-only power bank need RRA/EMC approval?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">No. Korea's Radio Research Agency excludes simple DC-in and DC-out batteries and power banks from radio-law conformity assessment under Notice Annex 1. A power bank that only takes DC power and delivers DC power does not need an RRA assessment for that function. A wireless-charging, Bluetooth, AC-output, or other radio-function variant does need a separate assessment. The exemption follows the function, not the product name.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What changes under the KC 62133-2 draft?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">The draft WTO notification G/TBT/N/KOR/1378 is dated 2026-08-27. It would add KC 62368-1 whole-unit safety for power banks. It would also redefine the scope as portable equipment up to 18 kg, exclude batteries of 500 Wh or more and drones, and make the internal short-circuit test substantive. The comment period closes on 2026-10-26. Final adoption and effective dates are still undetermined, so treat 2027 expectations as unconfirmed.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
